ation so he's undermining his own  argument not at all i've just explained  why because what she's saying is let's  borrow money to cut taxes in the hope  that it will stimulate demand further  well the problem at the moment is demand  is already outstripping supply which is  why we've got inflation what he's saying  is that let's try uh first of all we've  got the windfall uh tax on the energy  companies then let's look at public  spending projects to see if we can save  that money so that we can ensure that  the help is available to the vulnerable  only in a very last resort and for a  one-off reason would we want to see  further boring so there's quite a big  difference okay and so let's look at  what he is preparing to offer he's  talking about 10 billion pounds worth of  hell but 5 billion of that is that the  vat plan that he's already announced on  energy bill so are we talking about five  billion of new money and if so is that  enough well it's 10 billion of new help  um what he said is that that's already  been the summit's been announced but  it's not been spent and it's not uh the  money's not yet available in that sense  so  what it says is that yes we understand  rishi understands that there is a need  to help people further as long as this  energy price emergency continues  eventually of course prices are likely  to fall as global supply comes back up  to the levels that we had before but  there's going to be a period where help  will be required and it's responsible  socially responsible to ensure that that  help is available and how far will that  five billion of newly announced money  how far will that go the consumer  consumer champion martin lewis has  talked about the need to double the  amount of support that's available i  mean the last lot of new support was  worth about 15 billion so this this  isn't going to go far enough well that  supports some of that support of course  is is still coming through the pipeline  uh another 10 billion would 